# EMS Operations (NREMT AEMT test plan: 6% to 10%): vehicles and equipment, leadership and
# professionalism, communication and documentation, medical and legal standards, environment
# of care. Laws that vary by state are worded "per state law" or "per local protocol".
# Sources: NREMT AEMT examination specifications; 2019 National EMS Scope of Practice Model;
# HHS HIPAA Privacy Rule summary; CDC sequence for donning PPE; NASEMSO Model Guidelines v3.0
# (Universal Care, Mass Casualty Triage).
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    X(1, 2, "Patient privacy", "For each disclosure of patient information, specify whether HIPAA permits it.",
      ["Report to the receiving nurse", "His name to a TV reporter", "Scene photo on social media", "Abuse report required by state law", "His diagnosis to his boss"],
      ["Permitted", "Not permitted"], [0, 1, 1, 0, 1],
      "Sharing information for treatment, and reports the law requires, are permitted. Telling the media, posting photos or informing an employer without the patient's authorization are not."),
    S(1, 2, "Refusal of care", "An alert adult with capacity refuses transport after a minor crash. What should you do?",
      ["Explain the risks; document it", "Transport him against his will", "Leave without any paperwork", "Ask police to arrest him"],
      "An adult with decision-making capacity may refuse. Make sure he understands the risks, encourage him to call back, get a signature and document the assessment and discussion."),
    S(1, 3, "Scope of practice", "A paramedic on scene asks you to push IV midazolam for a seizure. What should you do?",
      ["Say it is outside your scope", "Give it, since he is senior", "Give half the dose", "Call medical direction and give it"],
      "Benzodiazepines are not in the AEMT scope model, and neither a senior provider's request nor a radio order widens your license. Offer the help you can give, such as airway support, and let the paramedic give the drug."),
    S(1, 2, "Medical direction", "Written standing orders an AEMT follows without a radio call are a form of:",
      ["Offline medical direction", "Online medical direction", "Local custom", "Mutual aid"],
      "Protocols and standing orders are offline (indirect) medical direction. A radio or phone order from a physician during the call is online medical direction."),
    S(1, 2, "Medication safety", "Online medical direction orders a drug over the radio. What prevents an error?",
      ["Repeat the order back", "Write it down after the call", "Have your partner guess", "Give it before confirming"],
      "Echoing the drug, dose and route back to the physician confirms the order was heard correctly before you give it. Writing it later or acting before confirming invites errors."),
    S(1, 3, "Mass casualty triage", "In SALT triage, which lifesaving action may be done before assigning a category?",
      ["Controlling major bleeding", "Starting an IV line", "Taking a full set of vitals", "Splinting a closed fracture"],
      "SALT allows quick lifesaving interventions during triage: control of major hemorrhage, opening the airway, chest decompression and antidote auto-injectors. IVs, full vitals and splints wait."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    O(2, 2, "Medication administration", "Place the steps for giving a medication in order.",
      ["Confirm the order or protocol", "Ask about allergies", "Check drug, dose and expiration", "Give the medication", "Reassess and document"],
      "Confirm the indication and order, check allergies, verify the drug, dose, route and expiration, give it, then reassess the effect and record it. Documenting before giving invites errors."),
    S(2, 2, "Hazardous materials", "At a chemical spill, where should an AEMT treat decontaminated patients?",
      ["Cold zone", "Hot zone", "Warm zone", "Downwind of the spill"],
      "Patient care happens in the cold zone after decontamination in the warm zone. The hot zone is for trained hazmat teams, and downwind exposes the crew."),
    S(2, 2, "Emergency driving", "Running lights and siren, you reach an intersection with a red light. What is safest?",
      ["Stop; clear each lane", "Keep going at speed", "Rely on the siren", "Pass on the right"],
      "Most crashes happen at intersections. Even where state law allows proceeding through a red light, come to a stop and clear each lane before crossing; other drivers may not hear the siren."),
    S(2, 1, "Documentation", "You find an error in a patient care report you wrote. How should you correct it?",
      ["One line, initial and date", "Black it out completely", "Rewrite the whole report", "Delete it with no note"],
      "A single line through the error, with your initials, the date and the correct entry, keeps the record honest. Erasing or rewriting looks like tampering."),
    S(2, 2, "Mandated reporting", "An older woman you transport has bruises in many stages and seems afraid of her caregiver. What fits?",
      ["Report it per state law", "Confront the caregiver", "Report it only if she asks", "Leave it off the chart"],
      "EMS providers are mandated reporters of suspected abuse in most states. Report through the required channel, document objective findings and avoid confronting the suspected abuser."),
    S(2, 2, "Exposure", "You are stuck by a used needle while starting an IV. What comes first?",
      ["Wash with soap and water", "Squeeze to make it bleed", "Finish the call, then report", "Apply bleach to the wound"],
      "Wash the site with soap and water at once, then report the exposure right away so testing and preventive treatment can start in time. Bleach and squeezing do not help."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    M(0, 2, "Refusal of care", "Which elements are needed for a patient's refusal to be valid? Select two.",
      ["Decision-making capacity", "Understanding of the risks", "A relative agrees", "A police officer signs", "The patient is over 65"],
      "A valid refusal needs a patient with capacity who has been told and understands the risks of refusing. A relative's agreement or a police signature does not make it valid."),
    S(0, 2, "Handoff", "What makes the best hospital handoff?",
      ["A brief structured report", "Every detail of the call", "Only the vital signs", "The chart left on the bed"],
      "A brief structured format, such as mechanism, injuries, signs and treatment, keeps key facts from being missed. Leaving only paperwork or listing every detail loses the message."),
    S(0, 1, "Consent", "An unresponsive adult needs care and no family is present. Which consent applies?",
      ["Implied consent", "Expressed consent", "Informed refusal", "Minor's consent"],
      "The law assumes an unresponsive person would agree to emergency care, so implied consent applies. Expressed consent comes from a patient who can speak for himself."),
    S(0, 2, "Advance directives", "A patient in cardiac arrest has a valid DNR order at the bedside. What is appropriate?",
      ["Withhold CPR; support family", "Start CPR until a doctor arrives", "Shock only once", "Call the police first"],
      "A valid DNR means resuscitation is withheld, while you still offer comfort and support to the family. Confirm the document follows your state's rules and local protocol."),
    S(0, 2, "Abandonment", "Which act could be called abandonment?",
      ["Leaving him with an EMR without a report", "Handing over to a paramedic with a report", "Transferring care at the ED", "Leaving after a signed refusal"],
      "Abandonment is ending care without transferring it to someone with equal or higher training. A documented transfer or a valid refusal is not abandonment."),
    S(0, 1, "Safe lifting", "How should you lift a loaded stretcher?",
      ["Keep it close; use your legs", "Bend at the waist; lift fast", "Twist to face the ambulance", "Hold it at arm's length"],
      "Keep the load close, back straight, and lift with the legs. Twisting or reaching while holding weight is a common cause of back injury in EMS."),
]
